.Dd November 6, 2026
.Dt PING 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ping ,
.Nm ping6
.Nd send ICMP and ICMPv6 echo requests
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 46Anqv
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl I Ar interface
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl p Ar byte
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl t Ar ttl
.Op Fl W Ar timeout
.Op Fl w Ar deadline
.Ar host
.Nm ping6
.Op Ar options
.Ar host
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is BusyBox's applet on every image with BusyBox: os-real and the systems
built from it, the GNU root among them.
It sends ICMP echo requests to
.Ar host ,
one a second whether or not the last was answered, and prints each
reply's line, with its size, its sequence number from 0, its hop limit and
its round-trip time:
.Pp
.Dl 64 bytes from 10.0.2.2: seq=0 ttl=255 time=0.412 ms
.Pp
It stops when
.Ar count
replies have come, or the wait after the last request is over, or the
deadline passes, or an interrupt comes, and then prints the statistics:
.Pp
.Dl 3 packets transmitted, 3 packets received, 0% packet loss
.Dl round-trip min/avg/max = 0.398/0.412/0.431 ms
.Pp
A send that fails ends
.Nm
with the error and no statistics.
.Pp
The host may be an IPv4 or IPv6 address or a name, which
.Xr getaddrinfo 3
resolves.
.Nm ping6
is
.Nm
with
.Fl 6 .
.Pp
Any account may run
.Nm .
A process that may not open a raw socket, one that is not root, gets
.Er EPERM
from
.Xr socket 2 ,
and
.Nm
then opens an ICMP datagram socket,
.Fn socket AF_INET SOCK_DGRAM IPPROTO_ICMP
or its IPv6 form.
The kernel gives that socket an echo identifier no other holds, writes it
into each request with the checksum, and hands the socket only the replies
that carry it, so any number of
.Nm
programs run at once.
The reply's hop limit comes from
.Dv IP_RECVTTL ,
or
.Dv IPV6_RECVHOPLIMIT
for IPv6.
Root keeps the raw socket, which sees every ICMP message: a message that is
no echo and no reply is reported on the standard error.
.Pp
Each request has 56 bytes of data, or the size of
.Fl s ,
and the first four hold the send time on the monotonic clock.
The requests are sent from the handler of a timer, one interval apart.
.Pp
Once the last request is sent,
.Nm
waits for the replies still out: for the timeout of
.Fl W
when none has come at all, and otherwise for twice the longest round trip,
at least a second.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-w deadline"
.It Fl c Ar count
Stop after
.Ar count
requests, once their replies have come or the wait for them is over.
.It Fl I Ar interface
Send from
.Ar interface ,
an interface's name or an address of this machine's.
A name binds the socket to the interface with
.Dv SO_BINDTODEVICE
.Pq Xr setsockopt 2 :
the requests leave by it and from its address, even to a host the
routing table reaches by another interface, which is then taken to be on
this interface's link, and it is the scope of a link-local IPv6 host.
An address binds the socket to that address, and the first line then
names it:
.Pp
.Dl PING 10.0.2.2 (10.0.2.2) from 10.0.2.15: 56 data bytes
.It Fl i Ar interval
Send a request every
.Ar interval
seconds, which may have a fraction, instead of every second.
An interval of 0 is as short as the timer goes.
.It Fl A
Send the next request as soon as a reply comes, as well as at each
interval.
.It Fl p Ar byte
Fill the data with
.Ar byte ,
in hexadecimal, instead of zeros.
.It Fl s Ar size
Send
.Ar size
bytes of data in each request, instead of 56.
A request past one datagram of the path MTU leaves in fragments, and its
reply is reassembled.
.It Fl t Ar ttl
Send each IPv4 request with the hop limit
.Ar ttl .
.It Fl W Ar timeout
Wait
.Ar timeout
seconds after the last request when no reply has come at all, instead of
ten.
.It Fl w Ar deadline
Stop when
.Ar deadline
seconds have passed, at most 2147, whatever has been sent or received.
The time is checked as each request is sent, so a long interval overruns
it.
.It Fl q
Print only the first line and the statistics.
.It Fl v
With a raw socket, report every ICMPv6 message, not only echo replies.
.It Fl 4
Use IPv4 for a name.
.It Fl 6
Use IPv6 for a name.
.It Fl n
Accepted and ignored: replies are printed by address.
.El
.Ss os-simple
os-simple has no BusyBox.
Its
.Pa /bin/ping
is the project's program,
.Pa utils/ping.c ,
which sends through the same ICMP datagram socket and prints in the form
Linux's iputils prints: a reply's sequence number is
.Ql icmp_seq= ,
from 1, the statistics end
.Ql 3 received, 0% packet loss, time 2003ms ,
and a name resolves only to an IPv4 address through
.Pa /etc/hosts ,
or
.Pa /hosts
when that file is absent, and then a DNS A query.
It takes the options above but
.Fl A ,
.Fl p
and
.Fl v ,
waits for one interval after the last request where that is longer than
twice the round trip, stops at a failed send only when it is the first,
and exits 2 on an
error that stops it.
With
.Fl W
0 it waits until an interrupt.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/resolv.conf
.It Pa /etc/hosts
Host names and addresses.
.It Pa /etc/resolv.conf
DNS server configuration.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 when a reply arrived, and with both
.Fl c
and
.Fl w ,
when
.Ar count
replies arrived.
It returns 1 when they did not, and for any error that stops it: an option
it does not take, a host it cannot resolve, a source address the kernel
refuses, or a send that fails.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl ping -c 3 192.168.0.1
.Dl ping -c 2 -W 1 github.com
.Dl ping -q -w 5 1.1.1.1
.Dl ping -6 -c 2 ::1
.Dl ping -c 2 ff02::1%eth0
.Dl ping -c 2 -I wlan0 192.168.0.1
.Dl ping -c 2 -I 10.0.2.15 10.0.2.2
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql ping: bad address 'HOST'
The host is neither an address nor a name the files or DNS answer for, or
the argument of
.Fl I
is neither an address nor the name of an interface.
.It Ql ping: bind: Address not available
The address of
.Fl I
is not one of this machine's.
.It Ql ping: sendto: MESSAGE
The kernel could not send a request, for the reason
.Xr strerror 3
gives.
.It Ql ping: can't create raw socket: MESSAGE
The kernel would open neither the raw socket nor the datagram one.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr nc 1 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socket 2
